text:
&nbsp;


# **life of enslaved people in the roman empire**


&nbsp;


## **what's inside this chapter**


&nbsp;


1. jobs and roles
2. city life vs estate labor
3. the constant threat of punishment
4. family and work in the household


&nbsp;


---


&nbsp;


## **1. jobs and roles**


&nbsp;


enslaved people lived **very different lives**.

there was no single "slave experience." it all depended on a few things:


- their **age**
- their **gender**
- where they **lived** (a city or the countryside)


&nbsp;


rural life and city life were **not the same at all**.


&nbsp;


they held many kinds of jobs. some did **hard, unskilled labor**. others worked as **artisans**, making things by hand. many helped out in shops for merchants and shopkeepers.


&nbsp;


some got training in skilled roles. they became:


- **teachers**
- **doctors**
- **musicians**
- **actors**


&nbsp;


others built public works like **bridges and roads**. a few even served as **imperial administrators**, running parts of the government.


&nbsp;


---


&nbsp;


## **2. city life vs estate labor**


&nbsp;


in the city, life could be **a little easier**.


&nbsp;


in a household, enslaved people had more advantages. they often avoided the **brutal physical labor** found in other places.


&nbsp;


but not everywhere was like that.


&nbsp;


**mines, quarries, and large estates** demanded harsh, exhausting work. on those big estates, over **one hundred enslaved persons** might labor at once.


&nbsp;


their lives there were hard:


- they wore **poor clothing**
- they faced **cruel treatment**
- many could not save enough money to **buy their freedom**


&nbsp;


---


&nbsp;


## **3. the constant threat of punishment**


&nbsp;


no matter where they lived, **danger was always there**.


&nbsp;


a slaveholder could punish them at any moment. slaveholders were afraid of **conspiracy and rebellion**, and that fear shaped everything about how enslaved people were treated.


&nbsp;


this constant threat created a culture of **uncertainty**. coercion and submission became the norm.


&nbsp;


enslaved people could be:


- **whipped**
- **beaten or tortured**
- **subjected to sexual abuse**


&nbsp;


the writer **petronius** captured part of this reality in his novel, *satyricon*, written in the first century ce. in it, a freed man named trimalchio speaks about his past:


> "still, i was my master's favorite for fourteen years. no disgrace in obeying your master's orders. well, i used to amuse my mistress too. you know what i mean; i say no more, i am not a conceited man."


&nbsp;


enslaved people who **ran away** faced severe punishment if caught. owners could:


- **brand them with a mark**
- force them to wear a **collar carrying the owner's name**


&nbsp;


---


&nbsp;


## **4. family and work in the household**


&nbsp;


the law denied enslaved people **official marriage rights**.


&nbsp;


still, many formed families. they had children. this often happened in **urban settings**, where households were larger.


&nbsp;


a slaveholder could **manipulate these relationships** for personal gain. there was no legal protection against this.


&nbsp;


enslaved children were put to work as well. their duties might be **simple tasks inside the house**.


&nbsp;


over time, an enslaved person might move into a different role within that same household. this was **not a promotion** in any free sense. it simply changed their daily tasks under the **same owner's control**.

table of contents


- the zimmermann telegram
- why mexico could not join
- the mexican revolution begins (1910–1913)
- the factions fight (1913–1914)
- villa's raid and the u.s. response (1916)
- the u.s. reacts and enters the war


&nbsp;

---


## the zimmermann telegram



on march 1, 1917, a secret message was made public. it was called the **zimmermann telegram**. this event made tensions between germany and the united states even worse.



germany's foreign minister, arthur zimmermann, had sent this message to mexico. he offered mexico a deal.


&nbsp;

**what happened before that:**



in 1916, there were many disturbances along the u.s.–mexico border. a man named francisco "pancho" villa was behind them. he attacked towns in the united states. the u.s. army sent thousands of troops to the border. their job was to track down villa.


&nbsp;

**germany's plan:**



germany saw an opportunity. it wanted mexico to cause more border trouble if war broke out with the united states. more disturbances would mean more u.s. troops stuck at the border. those troops could not be sent to fight in europe.


&nbsp;

**the promise made to mexico:**



in return, germany made a promise. if germany won the war, it would give mexico back three regions: **texas**, **new mexico**, and **arizona**. mexico had lost that territory in 1848 after a war with the united states.


&nbsp;

---


## why mexico could not join



in reality, mexico could not take part in this plan. even if it wanted to, it was too divided. in 1917, violent conflict raged among many revolutionary factions inside mexico. villa's forces were just one of them.


&nbsp;

to understand why, we need to look back at how the trouble started.


&nbsp;

---


## the mexican revolution begins (1910–1913)



in 1910, a liberal politician named **francisco madero** ran for president of mexico. he challenged **porfirio díaz**. díaz had held the presidency continuously since 1884. his regime was dictatorial. it favored wealthy landowners and business owners.


&nbsp;

díaz won the election. the vote was rigged. he then had madero arrested. this act sparked uprisings across the country.


&nbsp;

in 1911, díaz was forced to resign. he went into exile.


&nbsp;

madero was declared president. but he failed to deliver the reforms people expected. a new revolt broke out against his government. in 1913, madero was forced to give up his office.


&nbsp;

a general named **victoriano huerta** replaced him. huerta commanded rebellious forces within the mexican army. soon after taking power, he had madero executed.


&nbsp;

huerta tried to rule like a dictator. opposition rose quickly.


&nbsp;

---


## the factions fight (1913–1914)



**in the south:**



**emiliano zapata** led a rebellion. his followers were indigenous peasants. they wanted a program of land reform.


&nbsp;

**in the north:**



three men led the revolution: **venustiano carranza**, **alvaro obregon**, and **pancho villa**.


&nbsp;

**the u.s. gets involved:**



u.s. president woodrow wilson backed carranza. he supplied carranza's forces with munitions. wilson also used this moment to order u.s. marines into mexico. his stated goal was to prevent damage to u.s.-owned property. in april 1914, the u.s. navy took control of the mexican port of **veracruz**.


&nbsp;

**the fighting continues:**



throughout the summer of 1914, the rebel factions fought each other. each leader wanted to replace huerta. in july, huerta fled into exile.


&nbsp;

carranza declared himself president. villa and zapata objected.


&nbsp;

---


## villa's raid and the u.s. response (1916)



in march 1916, villa attacked the town of **columbus, new mexico**. this was retaliation for u.s. support of carranza.


&nbsp;

wilson responded by sending five thousand troops across the border. their mission: capture villa.


&nbsp;

villa evaded them. within a year, the u.s. troops had retreated home.


&nbsp;

this invasion damaged relations between the united states and carranza. he refused to accept what he saw as an insult to mexican sovereignty.


&nbsp;

by 1917, carranza was firmly in power. but rebellion continued. villa led fighters in the northern state of **chihuahua**. zapata led fighters in **morelos** in the south.


&nbsp;

---


## the u.s. reacts and enters the war



so when germany approached carranza with its offer of an alliance against the united states, he was not in a position to accept it. he did not have the control or the stability to do so.


&nbsp;

**the telegram goes public:**



but when the telegram became public, the united states was furious. the country was not yet at war. and germany was already planning how to break up its territory.


&nbsp;

**money and business push toward war:**



american banks had extended loans to the european allies. if germany won, those loans would go unpaid. so the banks supported u.s. involvement. businesses saw a different benefit. they hoped that if the united states helped the allies win, the allies would grant them greater access to global markets.


&nbsp;

**the decision:**



popular support for going to war against germany swelled fast.


&nbsp;

in **april 1917**, the united states entered the conflict on the side of the allied powers. the country was already angry over the sinking of the **lusitania** and the **sussex**. the zimmermann telegram added fuel to that fire.

