convergence on lima

san martín takes lima

rivals meet in guayaquil

bolívar finishes the job

a continent-wide vision

the holy alliance threatens

the monroe doctrine

the congress of panama and its limits

a contradictory legacy



&nbsp;

# convergence on lima

&nbsp;

the northern and southern forces of the south american independence movements met in peru.

peru was a firmly royalist viceroyalty. both sides made lima, the capital city, their target.

&nbsp;

from the north, one stream of revolutionary armies moved south. bolívar led them from the viceroyalty of new granada.

from the south, another stream moved north. san martín led them up from the viceroyalty of río de la plata and the newly independent republic of chile.



&nbsp;

# san martín takes lima

&nbsp;

san martín left chile in 1820. he brought both land forces and sailors on ships.

a scottish former naval officer named thomas cochrane helped him. cochrane was a mercenary. like other foreigners, he had joined the south american patriots on his own initiative to fight for independence.

&nbsp;

san martín launched a naval assault on royalist peru. his forces landed on its shores in september 1820.

the highlands stayed in royalist hands. but his arrival sparked an uprising along the coast. he slowly expanded his foothold. eventually, he occupied lima itself.

&nbsp;

on july 28, 1821, peruvian creoles in the city were forced to act. they declared independence. they accepted san martín as protector of peru.



&nbsp;

# rivals meet in guayaquil

&nbsp;

lima was now under san martín's military and civil rule. but royalist troops still controlled the vast peruvian hinterland. the people of lima saw him and his forces as foreign invaders.

&nbsp;

meanwhile, bolívar had moved on after liberating bogotá in 1819. he turned his armies toward ecuador. he made little progress against the remaining royalists there.

&nbsp;

san martín and bolívar were rivals for control of the independence movement. they decided to meet in guayaquil, ecuador.

in 1821, guayaquil's valuable port and naval base fell under bolívar's control. in 1822, he entered the city of quito and declared ecuador's independence.

&nbsp;

in july 1822, san martín set sail for guayaquil. he hoped to convince the port city's merchants to unite with lima. but bolívar had arrived earlier. he pressed his guayaquil supporters to join colombia instead. by the time san martín landed in the city, its possession was no longer part of their discussion.

&nbsp;

probably disillusioned, san martín met with bolívar behind closed doors. the talks were secret.

by the end, san martín decided to leave the completion of south america's liberation to bolívar. shortly after, he withdrew from the independence struggle. he went into a self-imposed exile in europe. he never returned.



&nbsp;

# bolívar finishes the job

&nbsp;

bolívar occupied lima in 1823. in february 1824, a peruvian congress named him dictator of peru.

&nbsp;

in august 1824, he won a significant victory against spanish forces at the battle of junín.

then, in december 1824, his skilled chief of staff antonio josé de sucre defeated a much larger spanish force at the battle of ayacucho.

&nbsp;

in 1825, royalist troops in upper peru accepted a general amnesty after their defeat by sucre. upper peru was renamed bolivia in honor of el libertador.



&nbsp;

# a continent-wide vision

&nbsp;

both bolívar and san martín had a continent-wide outlook. both supported close alliances among the newly independent nations. but they disagreed on government.

&nbsp;

san martín shared an admiration for the british constitutional monarchy with mexican conservatives.

bolívar rejected the mystical figure of the king. he believed the republican system was the best guarantee of stability.

&nbsp;

in 1822, gran colombia became the first spanish american nation to receive diplomatic recognition from the united states.



&nbsp;

# the holy alliance threatens

&nbsp;

the united states feared that european countries would use the latin american wars of independence as an excuse to intervene in the western hemisphere. such action would threaten the young nation's security and its commerce.

&nbsp;

in 1815, napoléon was defeated at waterloo. afterward, austria, prussia, and russia formed the holy alliance. its purpose was to protect european empires by discouraging revolution. france had been engulfed in one.

&nbsp;

at conferences in 1820 and 1821, the holy alliance declared its right to intervene in rebellions that threatened european monarchs. in 1820–1821, a rebellion in naples was crushed by invading austrian troops.

&nbsp;

the holy alliance had not intervened in 1820 to help fernando vii. at that time, spanish liberals had forced him to reinstitute the constitution of 1812.

but in 1822, they agreed to support an invasion of spain by france's louis xviii. the goal was to end the spanish revolutionary movement and restore the imprisoned fernando to the throne.

&nbsp;

the members of the holy alliance also intended to assert fernando vii's control over his rebellious american colonies.



&nbsp;

# the monroe doctrine

&nbsp;

the united states opposed such intervention. britain did too, because it carried on extensive trade with latin america.

&nbsp;

britain's foreign minister proposed a joint statement. it would warn france and the holy alliance against imposing their will on the former spanish colonies.

u.s. secretary of state john quincy adams rejected this idea. he considered it "more candid as well as more dignified" for the new country "to avow our principles explicitly" than to let the british take the lead.

&nbsp;

accordingly, in 1823, adams set forth the monroe doctrine. this was with the reluctant agreement of president james monroe.

the doctrine warned european nations to refrain from interfering with independent countries in the western hemisphere. it became a principle of u.s. foreign policy.

&nbsp;

bolívar was gratified by u.s. support. but he trusted british influence more to block spain's attempts to regain its american colonies. he did not give much importance to the monroe doctrine.

in 1824, great britain joined the united states in officially recognizing gran colombia. its representatives then obtained a significant loan from the london financial market.



&nbsp;

# the congress of panama and its limits

&nbsp;

in 1826, bolívar convened the congress of panama. he wanted to strengthen fraternal ties among the newly independent nations in former spanish america. he also wanted to adopt programs of mutual cooperation and create a permanent alliance.

&nbsp;

the united states, haiti, and brazil were conspicuously absent. but the main difficulty was not their absence. it was the internal fragility of the new nations themselves.



&nbsp;

# a contradictory legacy

&nbsp;

the legacy of the spanish american revolutions was contradictory. the new nations had broken free of spain. yet colonial social hierarchies persisted.

these hierarchies escalated into a social struggle among enslaved africans, indigenous groups, mestizos, pardos, and white people.

&nbsp;

provinces fought each other. after defeating royalist forces, the popular armies faced civil wars over a new postcolonial order.

&nbsp;

creole leaders like bolívar and san martín were not the only heirs of independence. the main postcolonial leaders were local military chieftains. they often forged alliances with wealthy creole landowners. together, they perpetuated their power.

# latin american independence


## table of contents

- the struggle for freedom
- brazil's unique path
- outside eyes on south america
- maria dundas graham calcott
- what graham's diaries reveal today



&nbsp;



&nbsp;



## the struggle for freedom



&nbsp;



latin american colonies wanted to **break free**.


they wanted freedom from their parent countries.



&nbsp;



those parent countries ran a **mercantilist system**.


that system blocked the growth of fast-growing colonial states.



&nbsp;



the independence movements were led by **well-educated elites**.


but they often faced resistance.



&nbsp;



a majority of the population was mixed-race.


many of them sided with the homeland.



&nbsp;



this lack of popular support created a problem.



&nbsp;



the white creole minority feared the mixed-race majorities.


they had to **negotiate at every step**.


they could not simply take power by force.



&nbsp;



liberators like **bolívar**, **san martín**, **pedro i**, and **iturbide** led these movements.


they received no significant help from outside sources.


many times they lacked a unified direction or strategy.



&nbsp;



they also faced practical problems:


- vast geographic distances
- natural obstacles (mountains, rivers)
- economic isolation of different regions
- cultural differences between regions



&nbsp;



all of this made it hard to **unite the areas** they were trying to free.



&nbsp;



&nbsp;



---



&nbsp;



&nbsp;



## brazil's unique path



&nbsp;



brazil took a **different path**.


it was different from other latin american countries.



&nbsp;



its independence showed more continuity with its colonial past.


no other nation in the region matched that continuity.



&nbsp;



after 1822, brazil was free of portuguese rule.


but it did not become a republic.


it became an **independent empire**.



&nbsp;



the first emperor was **pedro i**.


he was a prince of the portuguese royal house.


he was still an heir to the portuguese throne.



&nbsp;



portuguese-born men kept their power after independence.


they continued to control trade.


they held key positions in:


- the bureaucracy
- the army
- the church



&nbsp;



political life in imperial brazil was shaped by a **conservative-liberal split**.


this division reflected conflicting interests and ideals.


those conflicts came from different economic groups.



&nbsp;



independence did not bring major changes to colonial socioeconomic structures.


the new constitutional monarchy simply **regularized the status quo**.



&nbsp;



&nbsp;



---



&nbsp;



&nbsp;



## outside eyes on south america



&nbsp;



the independence movement drew great **outside interest** in the region.


that curiosity led to more foreign travel and writing.



&nbsp;



people in the united states and europe often learned about south america through these accounts.


travelers wrote down what they saw.


others read those writings back home.



&nbsp;



in the nineteenth century, european women rarely traveled for pleasure.


tourism was a twentieth-century invention.


so **female travelers were unusual**.



&nbsp;



&nbsp;



---



&nbsp;



&nbsp;



## maria dundas graham calcott



&nbsp;



**maria dundas graham calcott** came to south america in the 1820s.


she traveled with her husband.


he was a navy officer.



&nbsp;



she served as governess to pedro i's daughter.


that daughter was **princess maria da glória**.


she would later become queen of portugal.



&nbsp;



graham kept diaries from **1821 to 1823**.


these diaries offer a unique view of the brazilian court.


they cover the independence process.



&nbsp;



her accounts also show her wide-ranging interest in people's daily lives.


she wrote about:


- brazil (salvador and rio de janeiro)
- chile (santiago)



&nbsp;



in chile, graham studied **science and botany**.


her teacher was thomas cochrane.


he was a former british navy officer.


he was also a friend of the family.



&nbsp;



&nbsp;



---



&nbsp;



&nbsp;



## what graham's diaries reveal today



&nbsp;



graham wrote her reports for her own time.


she wanted to enlighten and entertain her contemporaries.


but **today**, her words offer new insights.



&nbsp;



her diaries reveal two things:


- gender relations in newly independent south american nations
- the elite social circles of the early nineteenth century



&nbsp;



graham focused on what she saw as **"suitable" subjects** for a woman.


these included fashion and motherhood.


they related to the lives of brazilian women.



&nbsp;



public life was largely male.


her diaries show that female foreign observers like herself remained **outsiders** in those spaces.



&nbsp;



graham was an urban elite woman with a protestant background.


in her writing, she held up civilized english customs as a model for the world.


that bias shaped what she noticed and how she described it.



&nbsp;



but despite that bias—or perhaps because of it—her accounts reveal much.



&nbsp;



through her view of private aspects of brazilian women's lives, we learn about **public events** in south american society.


we also see her own models of femininity at work.



&nbsp;



her travel narrative is a window into one woman's world.


it shows how she saw nineteenth-century brazilian women **shape their lives and their environment**.

table of contents


1. reparation


2. the trans-atlantic slave trade



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

# 1. reparation


**reparation** is a form of payment.


It makes up for past harm.


A group that was suppressed can receive this payment from the society that wronged them.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Most societies in the world have faced subjugation.


More powerful groups or classes imposed control over them.


Examples include:


- colonialism
- the jewish holocaust during world war ii
- the trans-atlantic slave trade (tast)



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

After a suppressed group gains freedom, they often seek reparation.


This payment can take two forms:


- **in kind** — goods or services
- **materialistic** — money or property



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

The most well-known and controversial case is reparation for slavery in africa.


The argument goes like this:


Descendants of africans who were enslaved during the tast deserve a compensatory payment.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

# 2. the trans-atlantic slave trade


The tast moved millions of africans to the new world.


In this trade, humans were treated as goods.


They were bought and sold like gold or ivory.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

European merchants carried africans across the atlantic ocean.


This began after the discovery of the new world.


The goal was to supply labour for plantations.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

This trade caused deep harm in two places:


**In africa:** local societies were devastated.


**In the diaspora:** enslaved people and their descendants suffered greatly.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Slaves were obtained through several means:


- warfare
- kidnapping
- sale from markets
- other sources



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

The suffering was severe at every stage:


1. the middle passage (the ocean crossing)
2. life on the plantation system
3. psychological trauma even after emancipation



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

These injustices affected slaves and their descendants in the americas.


Because of this, african-americans and members of the caribbean diaspora are increasingly calling for reparation.

